<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Socrates</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 470–<a href="/years/399-bce">399 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Socrates was an Athenian philosopher who wrote nothing himself but questioned his fellow citizens about justice, virtue and knowledge, a method of inquiry known through the dialogues of his pupil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>. In 399 BCE he was tried for impiety and corrupting the young, condemned and executed by drinking hemlock. He is regarded as a founder of Western philosophy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/socrates.jpg" alt="Socrates" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copy of Lysippos (?) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socrates_Louvre.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Socrates left no writings, and what is known of him comes from others: his pupils <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, the comic dramatist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, who was born after his death. These accounts often contradict one another, a difficulty scholars call the Socratic problem. Xenophon wrote about him in the Memorabilia, the Oeconomicus, the Symposium and the Apology. How far Plato's dialogues portray the historical Socrates is debated, and some scholars distinguish an earlier, more faithful portrait from a later one carrying Plato's own views.</p><p>Socrates was born in 470 or 469 BCE in the Athenian deme of Alopece, to Sophroniscus, a stoneworker, and Phaenarete, a midwife. He had the usual education of a well-off Athenian and served in three campaigns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>. He married Xanthippe, with whom he had three sons, and also a daughter of the statesman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a>. He was indifferent to comfort, owned one ragged coat and walked barefoot, and he criticised both the democrats and the oligarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The method named for him, the elenchus, is shown mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s early dialogues. Socrates asks an apparent expert for a definition, and further questions lead the expert to contradict it. A new definition is then tested in the same way. The discussion usually ends in an impasse, with the interlocutor unable to define what he thought he understood. Socrates frequently proclaimed his ignorance, saying he was sure only that he did not know.</p><p>Scholars dispute what the method achieves. In 1982 Gregory Vlastos argued that it could show only that an interlocutor's beliefs were inconsistent. Because Socrates taught no fixed doctrine, later schools drew different conclusions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 399 BCE Socrates was accused of impiety and of corrupting the youth. The accusation was brought by a poet, Meletus, with Anytus and Lycon. The trial came after the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aegospotami">Battle of Aegospotami</a> in 404 BCE and the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">Thirty Tyrants</a>, and it probably lasted most of a day. A jury of hundreds of citizens found him guilty. He proposed free meals from the state, or alternatively a fine of one mina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, and the jurors ordered his death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> relates that he was put to death with hemlock after refusing offers to help him escape.</p><p>Almost every philosophical current after Socrates traced its roots to him, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Academy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s Lyceum, the Cynics and the Stoics, except the Epicureans and the Pyrrhonists. He was considered to have shifted philosophy from the natural world to human affairs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Socrates” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Socrates</b>, tried and executed in 399 BCE, left his mark through his pupils; Plato founded the Academy around 387 BCE, Aristotle the Lyceum in 335 BCE, and Zeno and Epicurus opened their Stoic and Epicurean schools around 300 BCE, making Athens the philosophical capital of the Mediterranean into…</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Plato's Apology <b>Socrates</b> traces his philosophical mission to the Pythia's answer that no one was wiser than he.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Philosophers questioned the traditional gods: in the 6th century BCE Xenophanes mocked the idea of gods in human form, and in 399 BCE <b>Socrates</b> was condemned to death in Athens for impiety.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The full canon is described as taking in the Old and New Testaments, the Quran and works of Plato and of those influenced by <b>Socrates</b>, and Druze tradition counts Pythagoras, Plato and Aristotle among the five great prophets of the age of the ancient Greeks.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Aristotle himself warned that aristocracies slide into oligarchy when the few begin to rule for their own advantage, and in Plato’s Republic <b>Socrates</b> describes oligarchy as a city divided between the rich and the poor.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Plato, whose teacher <b>Socrates</b> was condemned to death by an Athenian jury of some 500 citizens in 399 BCE on charges of impiety and corrupting the young, compared the democratic city to a ship whose crew ignore the true navigator and seize the helm, and argued in the…</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-424" aria-label="Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Socrates"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-424">What was here in 424 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…daily to discourse about virtue, and of those other things about which you hear me examining myself and others, is the greatest good of man, and that the unexamined life is not worth living…&#8221;</p><footer>Apology &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; Plato's account of his defence &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1656/pg1656-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Well, although I do not suppose that either of us knows anything really beautiful and good, I am better off than he is,—for he knows nothing, and thinks that he knows; I neither know nor think that I know.&#8221;</p><footer>Apology &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; Plato's account of his defence &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1656/pg1656-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Crito, I owe a cock to Asclepius; will you remember to pay the debt?&#8221;</p><footer>Phaedo &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; his last words, in Plato's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1658/pg1658-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The hour of departure has arrived, and we go our ways—I to die, and you to live. Which is better God only knows.&#8221;</p><footer>Plato, Apology &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; his last words to the jury that condemned him, as Plato gives them &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1656/pg1656-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am that gadfly which God has attached to the state, and all day long and in all places am always fastening upon you, arousing and persuading and reproaching you.&#8221;</p><footer>Plato, Apology &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; his defence at his trial, as Plato gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1656/pg1656-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Wherefore, O judges, be of good cheer about death, and know of a certainty, that no evil can happen to a good man, either in life or after death.&#8221;</p><footer>Plato, Apology &#183; tr. Benjamin Jowett, 1871 &#183; to the jurors who had voted to acquit him, as Plato gives it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1656/pg1656-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
